      "thesis": "The memory complex has spent six weeks arguing with its own fundamentals. Micron closed 10-Aug at $861 — roughly 29% below the $1,213.37 all-time-high close of 25-Jun — after the July swoon briefly put the group in an official bear market. Against that price action stands a sourced supply-demand picture that has only tightened: all three HBM vendors are sold out for 2026 and 2027 capacity is FULLY ALLOCATED; Goldman models 2027 HBM ASPs up 87% (Samsung) to 100% (SK Hynix); Micron's DRAM share has climbed to 25%, one point behind SK Hynix's 26%; and the demand side was just re-confirmed by the hyperscalers' ~$725bn (+77%) 2026 capex and Microsoft's FY27 $255-260bn disclosure. The tell for what the market actually fears is SK Hynix: its ADR — July's record $26.5bn listing — trades at $135, BELOW the $149 IPO price, despite record Q2 revenue of KRW 79.3tn (+257% YoY). Blowout numbers, falling stock: the market is pricing PEAK PRICING, i.e. a 2027 supply response (Samsung and SK Hynix capacity warnings are the named risk), not absent demand. The desk's edge is not to out-argue that debate with direction — it is to monetise it: post-swoon implied vol on MU is rich, so a Phoenix autocall or cash-secured puts at a $700-730 barrier pays a double-digit coupon for agreeing to buy the stock ~35-40% below the ATH, a level that already prices a hard landing in 2027 ASPs. NVIDIA's 26-Aug print is the near catalyst for the whole chain; Micron's own FQ4 lands late September. This supersedes the old post-print idea (mu-fq3-react) — the argument has changed from 'monetise the gap after a record print' to 'own the dislocation at a discount, paid'.",
